{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791237043,"nonce":"QuBqQaqWRe2p+6tYYkhXytbdn/U=","title":"An optional parameter typed as an abstract over Int is not nullable (Issue2224)","message":"static function getEF2(?opt:haxe.EnumFlags\u003cMyEnum\u003e) return 'opt=$opt';\n    getEF2(); // \"opt=0\", Haxe \"opt=null\"\n\nTwo gaps:\n- `optional_param_type` wrapped `?x:T` in Null\u003cT\u003e only for a bare Int/Float/Bool, not for an abstract whose underlying type is one.\n- An instantiated abstract (`EnumFlags\u003cMyEnum\u003e`) has `underlying: None`; only the declaring type carries it. Both that rule and MIR's `optional_inner_is_boxable_primitive` read the field directly, so `Null\u003cEnumFlags\u003cE\u003e\u003e` holding 0 was passed unboxed and printed `null`.\n\nAlso: `resolve_abstract_underlying` cached a miss forever, so asking before the abstract was lowered poisoned later lookups. And the handle-abstract exclusion (Usize/Ptr/Ref/Box/Atomic/SIMD) matched bare names, so a user `abstract Box(Int)` was treated as rayzor.Box; it now matches qualified names.\n\nIssue2224 passed on Linux only while the stdlib was missing (6b4d7ae).","files":null}]}